1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
In this lesson, we're going to create our base email assistant.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
It's not going to have any memory yet.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
We're just going to set it up and get it working.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
All right, let's dive in.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The first thing we're going to want to do is import some environment variable.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
After that, we're going to start to set up our profile for this email assistant.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
The first part of this is just basic facts about the user,

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
like their name and some background about them.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
This should be a profile for the users whose email this assistant will be attempting to respond to.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
So you can change this to be whatever you want, probably your name and your background.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
After that, we're going to define some prompt instructions.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
These are instructions that will get formatted into a larger prompt down below.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
There's two parts of this.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The first is triage rules.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
These are parts that will go into the triage prompt.

16
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The triage prompt is what is responsible for triaging the user's email into a few different categories.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
There are three to be precise.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Ignore, notify, and respond.

19
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And we can see here that we have instructions for what type of emails should be classified into each of these categories.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A second instruction set is instructions for the main agent.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
This main agent gets called after we decide that we should respond to the email.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
So this contains instructions for what to do in that case.

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
For both the profile and the prompt instructions, these will get formatted into larger prompts down below.

24
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The reason that we're pulling them out is twofold.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
One, this makes it more modular and easier to see and visualize.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Two, in the future, some of these will actually be generated as part of memory.

27
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And so we want them to be separate from the rest of the prompt, which we are not going to update as part of memory.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
After that, let's define an example email.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
This is just to see what the schema of the emails that we'll be working with are.

30
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:21,000
There's four main parts, a from email address, a to email address, a subject for that email, and then the body.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
We'll use this to test out some of the agent parts down below.

32
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
You can change this to be whatever you want.

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Let's now define the first part of the agent.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
This will be the triage step.

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
First, we're going to set up some imports.

36
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Next, we're going to choose the model that we want to use for this triage step.

37
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:55,000
We're going to use OpenAI's GPT-40 mini model, but you can use whatever one you want.

38
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
After that, we'll define the schema that we want this triage step to output.

39
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
We'll use a pedantic base model here and it will have two fields.

40
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
First, we'll be a reasoning field.

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
This is where the LLM can just generate some reasoning for what it decided to do.

42
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
And then next is the main part, the classification step.

43
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
This should be one of ignore, respond, and notify.

44
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
We're going to bind this type to the LLM with with structured output.

45
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:36,000
This will make it so that LLM router always returns information in this schema.

46
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Now we're going to import some prompts for the triage step.

47
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
There's two that we're going to be working with, the system prompt and the user prompt.

48
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Let's take a look at the system prompt.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
We can see here that it's got a few different sections.

50
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
First is a role section.

51
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
This is where some background about who the AI should be is placed.

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Then we have the background on the user themselves.

53
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Then we have some instructions.

54
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
This defines the three different categories of things that we want to classify emails into.

55
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And then we have a rule section.

56
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
This is where we will format a lot of our instructions into.

57
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
And so you can see the here that there are these little curly brackets.

58
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
This is actually a prompt template.

59
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
This means that there are parts of this prompt that are not filled in.

60
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And we're going to do that with different variables and we'll see how to do that.

61
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
And then finally we can see that there's a section for few shot examples.

62
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
If we look at the user prompt, we can see that it's much simpler.

63
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:46,000
We can see that basically just formats the email into a pretty simple input.

64
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Let's test this out.

65
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
We can see here that we call triage system prompt dot format.

66
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
This will format the prompt template with a bunch of variables that we pass in.

67
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:03,000
We use the information in profile, such as the full name, the name, and the user profile background to populate some things.

68
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Examples we set as none because we don't have any of these yet.

69
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And then the triage no triage notify and triage email parts we take from the prompt instructions.

70
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:23,000
We do the same with the user prompt formatting it with information from the example email that we have.

71
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
With that done, we can now call the LLM router, which is this LLM with the router schema attached.

72
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
We pass in two messages, a system message with a system prompt as content.

73
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
And then a user message with the user prompt as content.

74
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
If we take a look at what result is, we can see that there's two parts.

75
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
There's a reasoning string. This is the logic of the LLM.

76
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
And then there's a classification of the LLM. In this case, it's saying to respond.

77
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Now's a good time to pause and try it out with some different emails or different instructions and see what you get.

78
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
With the logic of the triage step out of the way, we're now going to tackle the main agent.

79
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
First, we're going to define some tools. So we're going to import the tool wrapper from link chain.

80
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
The first tool we're going to define is a tool for writing emails.

81
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:24,000
This tool will take in three things, an email address to send the email to a subject of the email and the content of the email.

82
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Right here, we just have a fake dummy tool.

83
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
So we're not actually implementing any logic in it.

84
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
In the real world, you would connect this to your Gmail API or Outlook API to actually send the email.

85
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
But here, we're just going to mock it out.

86
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
The next tool is a schedule meeting tool.

87
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:51,000
This is going to take in a list of attendees, a subject, a duration, and a preferred day, and it will schedule a calendar meeting.

88
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Again, this is just a mock tool for now. In the real world, you would connect this to real APIs.

89
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
And the final tool we're going to define is a tool that will check the calendar availability.

90
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
So we'll take in a day and it will return the availability for the user on that day.

91
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Again, we're just mocking this tool out. So we're always going to return 9 a.m., 2 p.m., and 4 p.m.

92
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:24,000
With our tools defined, we're now going to go ahead and create the prompt for this agent.

93
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:31,000
So this is actually going to be a function that takes in the state of the agent and returns a list of messages.

94
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
This list of messages is going to consist of two parts. One will be the messages already in the state,

95
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and then the second will be a system message that we put at the start.

96
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,000
What is exactly is in this system message?

97
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,000
If we print out the prompt, we can see that there is a little bit about the role that the AI is taking on.

98
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:59,000
There's then a section that lists out the tools that it has access to, and then there's a section for the custom instructions.

99
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And we're going to pull these in from the prompt instructions that we defined above.

100
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Let's now build the agent.

101
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:17,000
We're going to use create react agent, which is an off-the-shelf agent implementation.

102
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,000
We're going to create this agent by calling create react agent and passing in three things.

103
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
First is a string corresponding to the model to use.

104
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
In this case, we're going to use open AI and the GPT-4L model.

105
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Next, we're going to pass in a list of tools.

106
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
In this case, the three tools we have defined above.

107
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
And then finally, we're going to pass in the prompt.

108
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
In this case, the function for creating the prompt that we have defined above.

109
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
We can now try it out.

110
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
So we're going to invoke it with a list of messages.

111
00:08:48,000 --> 00:09:01,000
And in this case, let's pass in something like what's my availability for Tuesday?

112
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
We'll get back a response.

113
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
This response will have a list of messages as part of the state.

114
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
If we print out the last message in this list of messages, we can see that it responds with you have the following available time slots on Tuesday.

115
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
9am, 2pm, and 4pm.

116
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Great!

117
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:28,000
If you remember, these are the times that we hard-coded our calendar tool to say that we were available.

118
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Let's now put it all together and create the overall agent.

119
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
The first thing we're going to do is define the state of the agent.

120
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Here the state is going to consist of two parts.

121
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
First is an email input. This is what the user will pass in.

122
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
This will contain all the information about the email.

123
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Second will be a list of messages.

124
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,000
This is where the agent will do its work.

125
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:01,000
After defining the state, let's import some things that we'll use to build the rest of the agent.

126
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,000
We're going to first define a node for doing the triage step.

127
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
This is going to take in the state of the agent and it's going to return a command.

128
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
A command is going to say two things.

129
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
We'll see this later on, but it will say two things.

130
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
It will return an update to the state and then it will say where to go.

131
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
These two values here are possible values of where to go after this triage node.

132
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
We could either go to the response agent or we could go to the end of the graph.

133
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
This is going to depend on the result of the triage step.

134
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
So let's actually implement this logic.

135
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:43,000
The first thing we're going to do is pull apart the email input into a few different things

136
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
that we're going to pass into the prompt down below.

137
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:50,000
This is just done for convenience. There's no real logic here.

138
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Next, we're going to create the system prompt.

139
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
This is the same as above.

140
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
After that, we're going to create the user prompt.

141
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,000
This is using the author and two and subject and email thread information from the email input.

142
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
We're then going to call the LLM router that we have defined above.

143
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Make sure this is LLM router not LLM. This is the one with the output that will have the classification schema.

144
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
We're then going to handle the three different types of classification that can occur.

145
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Respond, ignore, and notify.

146
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
If it's something else, we're going to raise a value error. That's unexpected.

147
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
At the end, we're going to return this command.

148
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:40,000
This basically tells the graph which node to go to and which update to apply.

149
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:50,000
So each of these branches for each of these classification results should set a go to value and an update value.

150
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Let's take a look at respond.

151
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
This is what happens when we want to respond to the email.

152
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
First, we're going to print out some nice stuff. This is just so that we can see what's going on.

153
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Our go to is going to be response agent.

154
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
So we want to go to the response agent afterwards.

155
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
The update we're going to apply is we're going to add a new message to this list of messages.

156
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:20,000
It's going to be a user message and it's going to have this content respond to the email and then it's going to paste in the email input there.

157
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
When the LLM calls ignore, we're first going to print out something.

158
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
This is similarly just for logging.

159
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Our update is going to be none.

160
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
So we're not going to update the state at all.

161
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,000
And then we're going to call go to we're going to go to the end node.

162
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
What if it's notify?

163
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Here for mocking, it's actually going to be pretty similar to ignore.

164
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
We're going to print something out with a slightly different print statement.

165
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And then we're still going to apply no update and we're going to go to the end of the graph.

166
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
In real life, this would probably do something else.

167
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
This would go to some sort of notification system.

168
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
So you might want to go to a different node if you add that into the graph.

169
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
But here we don't have that.

170
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
So we're just going to do a simple go to the end.

171
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:06,000
And that completes our triage node, pretty complex, but hopefully you understand.

172
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:15,000
At a high level, it's basically formatting a prompt, calling the LLM, and then based on the output, deciding which node to go to next.

173
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Let's put it all together and define our email agent.

174
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
So we're going to use a state graph, which is an agent that operates on a particular state.

175
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
The state that is going to operate is on the state that we defined above.

176
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
So it has an email and a list of messages.

177
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
We're going to add two nodes, the triage node, which we defined above.

178
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:42,000
And then the response agent, which is a sub agent, this is the react agent that we first defined.

179
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
We're going to add an edge from start to the triage router.

180
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
This is basically just saying that the first node we want to go into after the start is this triage step.

181
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And then we're going to compile this agent.

182
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Before using it, let's check out what it looks like.

183
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:07,000
So we can use this draw mermaid PNG method on the LLM graph object to get a nice mermaid drawing of what it looks like.

184
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
And we're going to pass this x-ray equals true.

185
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:14,000
This is just allowing us to zoom into this response agent.

186
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
Because this is a sub agent itself, so we want to visualize it.

187
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
So if we look at the graph, we can see that we first go to this triage step.

188
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:25,000
From there, either goes to end or goes into the response agent.

189
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
This response agent is, of course, an agent itself.

190
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:34,000
It's got this nice little react loop here of an LLM calling something and then calling some tools and then going back.

191
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Or it can finish.

192
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Let's try it out on a few emails.

193
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
So first, here's one that looks like some spam.

194
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
It's spamming some nice sales email.

195
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Hopefully, this will be ignored by the email agent.

196
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Let's call it with this email input.

197
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
And we can see that it prints out this classification ignore.

198
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
So here we can tell that it's successfully ignoring this email.

199
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Let's try out another example.

200
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
This time, one that expects a response.

201
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
If we call it, we can see that we print out this classification respond.

202
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
This means that we're getting a response.

203
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:23,000
But what exactly is happening in order to see that,

204
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
let's take a look at the list of messages.

205
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
First, we can see the human message.

206
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:33,000
This is the human message that the triage node puts in to the list of messages.

207
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
We can then see that it calls the right email tool.

208
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:41,000
And here, it responds to Alice thanking her for reaching out.

209
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:46,000
We can then see that there's a tool message basically just saying that we sent this message.

210
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
And then we can see a final AI message saying,

211
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
I've responded to Alice's email blah, blah, blah.

212
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
This is the complete email agent in action.

213
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:01,000
It first triages the email and then it handles it accordingly if it's meant to.

214
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Now is a good time to pause and try out some other emails,

215
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:10,000
try out some other instructions, try out messing around with the system prompt and things like that.

216
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
This concludes building the email agent,

217
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
but it still doesn't have any memory.

218
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,000
And so that's what we're going to add in in the next lessons.
